domain StructuredSemanticAuthoringKernelDesignV1Revision4Review:
  description "Evidence-chain review of exact D11 design candidate revision four."
problem P1:
  |
    Determine whether revision four closes every provider-timeout recovery branch while
    preserving accepted resource-outcome and information-gaining-recovery semantics.
evidence E1:
  |
    Exact review subject Seal 7bfc10a07b45203279dc44605df0c4f21c3da1a31dd4776a769c10544907229b
    matches the workfile whose SHA256 is
    559c8bce9165b51a3aa274c701ee17aee3fe97a96652601cb8b2f7fc71537594.
evidence E2:
  |
    Revision four lines 523 through 540 atomically moves a timed-out request out of
    outstanding, rejects late application, gives an admitted recovery a distinct
    identity, and requires exactly one typed recoveryBasis. This resolves the original
    request-fencing portion of the revision-three finding.
evidence E3:
  |
    Revision four lines 470 through 478 says exhaustion of a cumulative-resource
    dimension stops new work and returns the matching bounded-resource outcome.
evidence E4:
  |
    Revision four lines 542 through 545 maps failed cumulative-resource admission for
    transport recovery to provider_transport_unavailable rather than the matching
    bounded-resource outcome.
evidence E5:
  |
    Foundation v3 F15 lines 335 through 342 forbids new work when the required budget
    is unavailable. Its safe-stop contract lines 360 through 366 requires the failure
    receipt to identify the stop category and consumed budget. ADR-0032 lines 90 through
    91 requires call, token and cost ceilings to terminate their own failure modes.
evidence E6:
  |
    Revision four lines 531 through 540 permits recovery only with a typed basis proving
    a changed transport condition or materially different authorized request. Lines
    542 through 549 define terminal behavior for zero policy, consumed recovery, failed
    resource admission, and lost fence, but not for inability to establish either
    recovery basis while the current fence and budgets remain valid.
evidence E7:
  |
    Foundation v3 F9 lines 232 through 258 forbids unchanged blind retry, permits
    infrastructure retry after conditions change, and allows an attempt to fail after
    applicable recovery is exhausted.
evidence E8:
  |
    Revision four lines 758 through 765 says the fixture covers inadmissible recovery
    and no-recovery technical failure, but a fixture cannot choose whether the missing
    no-basis branch is provider failure, another recovery, or another state transition.
premise C1:
  |
    A failure category controls diagnostics and source-based retry evidence. Replacing a
    resource-exhaustion category with a provider-unavailability category loses the
    accepted reason even if both share the durable run state failed.
premise C2:
  |
    A closed recovery design must state the next transition when an explicit scheduling
    precondition cannot be met; tests cannot supply missing normative behavior.
premise U1:
  |
    Numeric timeout fitness, route behavior, and runtime implementation remain
    unverified and are outside this document review.
decision D1 based_on P1, E2:
  |
    Revision four resolves the revision-three late-result and replacement-request
    identity defect.
decision D2 based_on P1, E3, E4, E5, C1:
  |
    Mapping failed cumulative-resource admission to provider_transport_unavailable is
    a direct internal and F15/ADR-0032 contract conflict. The run must retain the timeout
    receipt but terminate with the matching bounded-resource outcome and consumed-budget
    evidence.
decision D3 based_on P1, E6, E7, E8, C2:
  |
    The no-valid-recoveryBasis branch remains undefined. When the current fence remains
    valid but neither accepted basis can be established, the same attempt cannot
    schedule a blind retry and must reach an explicit recoverable technical failure and
    owner new-run recipe rather than remain claimed without work.
decision D4 based_on P1, E1, D1, D2, D3, U1:
  |
    Report REVISE with two P1 findings for exact revision four. Do not modify the design
    or infer implementation, provider, cutover, or deployment authority from review.
